[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186120250 :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(5,9,-6)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $3x+2y+7z=-9.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by picking a point on the plane. To do that, we simply substitute some concrete values for $x$ and $y$ into the equation and solve for $z.$ Let's choose $x=2$ and $y=3$ (though it doesn't really matter what values we choose). Solving for $z,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2y+7z & =-9 \\\\ 3(2)+2(3)+7z & =-9 \\\\ 7z & =-21 \\\\ z & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(2,3,-3)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨2,3,-3⟩-⟨5,9,-6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,-6,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane $ax+by+cz=d,$ the normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩.$ So, for the plane $Π,$ we have $n=⟨3,2,7⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|(-3)\\cdot 3+(-6)\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 7|}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{7}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-9-12+21|}{\\sqrt{9+4+49}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\,0\\,|}{\\sqrt{62}} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $P$ lies on the plane." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186117659 :answer-field/correct 17592186117662 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "activity-efa2f5ef-e6fe-56c3-a086-47568aefef07" :activity/id #uuid "efa2f5ef-e6fe-56c3-a086-47568aefef07" :activity/title "The Shortest Distance Between a Plane and a Point" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049322 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/ste